<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edmund Ironside</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 990–<a href="/years/1016">1016</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Edmund II of England</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edmund Ironside was king of the English for seven months in 1016, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-the-unready">Æthelred the Unready</a>. He resisted Cnut's invasion vigorously, earning his nickname, but was defeated at the Battle of Assandun in October 1016. By the agreement that followed he kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> while Cnut took the lands north of the Thames, but Edmund died on 30 November 1016 and Cnut became king of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edmund-ironside.jpg" alt="Edmund Ironside" width="320" height="293" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edmund_Ironside_-_MS_Royal_14_B_VI.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Edmund was the third of the six sons of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-the-unready">Æthelred the Unready</a> and his first wife. He was probably born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> and raised by his grandmother Ælfthryth. His elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelstan">Æthelstan</a>, who was close to him, died in June 1014 and left Edmund the sword of Offa and estates in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danelaw">Danelaw</a>, as well as his connections in the Five Boroughs.</p><p>In early 1015, at an assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, Edmund's allies Sigeferth and Morcar were tricked and executed by Eadric Streona, with the complicity of Æthelred. Edmund rescued Sigeferth's widow from the monastery at Malmesbury and married her against the king's will. He occupied the Five Boroughs, and in one of two charters he called himself king. The revolt ended when Cnut invaded in the summer of 1015, and Edmund's early attempts to raise armies against him collapsed amid mistrust.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Æthelred died on 23 April 1016. The citizens and councillors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> chose Edmund as king, while most of the English magnates, meeting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southampton">Southampton</a>, elected Cnut. Edmund fought several battles with Cnut, at Penselwood, Sherston and Brentford, and probably Otford, and relieved the siege of London. His decision to take Eadric back into his ranks was heavily criticised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a>. At Assandun he was backed by the whole English nation, but Eadric fled, which caused a decisive English defeat.</p><p>The English magnates then pressed for peace, and Edmund met Cnut on an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-severn">River Severn</a> at Alney. They exchanged hostages, declared their friendship and fixed a payment for the Danish army. Edmund kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a> and Cnut took the north. Edmund died on 30 November after ruling for 222 days, and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glastonbury-abbey">Glastonbury Abbey</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Cnut secured all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, executed Eadric Streona in 1017 and exiled or executed Edmund's family and supporters. He then married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emma-of-normandy">Emma of Normandy</a>. His dynasty lasted until 1042, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a> restored the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Wessex</a>. Medieval writers praised Edmund as an indefatigable warrior, contrasting him with his father, and modern historians generally regard him as a capable leader who fought Cnut to a standstill.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edmund Ironside” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Edmund Ironside</b>, son of Æthelred the Unready, became king of the English and spent his seven-month reign fighting a Danish invasion led by Cnut the Great.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-23">23 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A Danish army led by King Cnut defeated the English under King <b>Edmund Ironside</b> at Assandun, in Essex.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-18">18 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Cnut, son of the Danish king Sweyn Forkbeard, conquered England in 1016 after the death of <b>Edmund Ironside</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-sea-empire">North Sea Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Kingdom of Wessex</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.75,-1.26&amp;year=1006" aria-label="Oxford on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4522 374 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Edmund Ironside"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4972 644h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4972 644h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.75,-1.26&amp;year=1006">What was here in 1006? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
